messages:    9%|9         | 19/204 [00:03<00:30,  6.04it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the information provided and analyze it step by step:.\n\n1.**If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( W \\rightarrow M \\), where \\( W \\) represents "working in student jobs on campus" and \\( M \\) represents "need to earn money to help pay for college tuition.".\n\n2.**If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.**.\n   - This is another conditional statement: \\( T \\rightarrow W \\), where \\( T \\) represents "ordering takeout frequently in college.".\n\n3.**People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.**.\n   - This is a disjunction: \\( T \\lor D \\), where \\( D \\) represents "enjoying the dining hall meals and recipes.".\n\n4.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( D \\rightarrow \\neg P \\), where \\( P \\) represents "being picky eaters.".\n\n5.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - This is another conditional statement: \\( D \\rightarrow E \\), where \\( E \\) represents "spending a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls.".\n\n6.**If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - This is a conditional statement: \\( W \\land M \\rightarrow \\neg P \\land \\neg M \\), where \\( W \\) and \\( M \\) are as defined above.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the given statement: "Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.".\nThe statement "Mary does not need to earn money to help pay for her college tuition" is false.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
