Q (John does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding).\n\n6.**If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then John has a wedding in July or, if not, then John travels to Paris for their honeymoon.**.\n   - This is another conditional statement: If R (John has a wedding that is well-attended), then S (John has a wedding in July) or T (John travels to Paris for their honeymoon).\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the implications:.\n\n- From the first statement, if John has a wedding, he must have at least one child.\n- From the second statement, if John has a wedding, he must travel to Paris for his honeymoon.\n- From the third statement, if John has a wedding in July, he must travel to Paris for his honeymoon.\n- From the fourth statement, if John has a wedding that is well-attended, he must either have a wedding in July or travel to Paris for his honeymoon.The statement "John has at least one child" must be true.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
